¶
'''wheme†''', ''a.'' and ''v.'' An obsolete variant of ''queme.''

'''whemmel, whemmle''' (hwem′l), ''v. t.'' [Also ''whammel'', Sc. ''quhemle'', ''whamle'', ''whommel'', a freq. (or perhaps orig. transposed) form of ''whelm.''] To whelm. [Prov. Eng. and Scotch.]

'''whemmel, whemmle''' (hwem′l), ''n.'' An overturn; an overthrow. [Scotch.]
{{smaller block|Nae doubt — ay, ay — it ’s an awfu’ ''whummle'' — and for ane that held his head sae high, too. {{right|''Scott'', Rob Roy, xxii.}}}}

'''when''' (hwen), ''adv.'' and ''conj.'' [< ME. ''when'', ''whan'', ''whon'', ''qvan'', ''qven'', ''qwan'', ''wan'', ''won'', ''hwon'', ''whenne'', ''whanne'', ''hwenne'', ''hwanne'', ''hwonne'', ''wenne'', ''wanne'', ''wonne'', ''wane'', ''wone'', < AS. ''hwænne'', ''hwonne'', when, = OS. ''hwan'' = OFries. ''hwenne'' = MD. ''wan'' = OHG. MHG. ''wanne'', ''hwanne'', G. ''wann'', when, ''wenn'', when, if, = Goth. ''hwan'', when; orig. a case of the interrog. pron. (cf. Goth. ''hwana'', acc. masc.), Goth. ''hwas'' = AS. ''hwā'', etc., who? see ''who''. Cf. L. ''quum'', ''quom'', when, as related to L. ''quis'', who? Gr. πότε, when? from same pron. base. Hence ult. ''whenne''<sup>2</sup>, ''whence''.] '''I.''' ''interrog. adv.'' At what time? at which time?
{{smaller block|''When'' shall these things be? and what shall be the sign of thy coming? {{right|Mat. xxiv. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>One [window] to the west, and counter to it,
And blank; and who shall blazon it? ''when'' and how?</poem>{{right|''Tennyson'', Holy Grail.}}}}
{{smaller block|''When'' was formerly used exclamatorily, like ''what'', to express impatience.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Why, ''when'', I say? . . .
Off with my boots, you rogues! you villains, ''when?'' . . .
Out, you rogue! you pluck my foot awry.</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., iv. 1. 146.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Why, ''when?'' begin, sir: I must stay your leisure.</poem>{{right|''Middleton'', More Dissemblers besides Women, v. 1.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|<poem>Set, parson, set; the dice die in my hand.
''When'', parson, ''when!'' what, can you find no more?</poem>
{{right|''Munday'' (''and others''), Sir John Oldcastle, iv. 1.}}}}

'''II.''' ''rel. conj.'' '''1.''' At the or any time that; at or just after the moment that; as soon as.
{{smaller block|''Whan'' Gawein saugh hem come, he seide now may we a-bide to longe. {{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 587.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''When'' the broken arches are black in night,
And each shafted oriel glimmers white, . . .
Then view St. David’s ruin’d pile.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', L. of L. M., ii. 1.}}}}
'''2.''' At which time.
{{smaller block|I am at London only to provide for Monday, ''when'' I shall use that favour which my Lady Bedford hath afforded me, of giving her name to my daughter.
{{right|''Donne'', Letters, xiii.}}}}
{{smaller block|The Moors fought valiantly for a short time, until the alcaydes of Marabella and Casares were slain, ''when'' they gave way and fled for the rear-guard.
{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 79.}}}}
{{smaller block|A time ''when'' the idols of the market-place are more devoutly worshipped than ever Diana of the Ephesians was.
{{right|''Lowell'', Harvard Anniversary.}}}}
{{smaller block|''When'' in this sense is sometimes used with ellipsis of ''the time'' preceding.}}
{{smaller block|I knew ''when'' seven justices could not take up a quarrel.
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, v. 4. 103.}}}}
{{smaller block|They were apprehended, and expected euer ''when'' to be put to death. Quoted in ''Capt. John Smith’s'' Works, I. 213.}}
'''3.''' At the same time that; whereas; while on the contrary: used adversatively, to denote contrast or incompatibility.
{{smaller block|<poem>You rub the sore,
''When'' you should bring the plaster.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, ii. 1. 139.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>How shall I please thee, how deserve thy smiles,
''When'' I am only rich in misery?</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Knight of Burning Pestle, ii. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|How then can any man be as a Witness, ''when'' every man is made the Accuser?
{{right|''Selden'', Table-Talk, p. 38.}}}}
{{smaller|''When'' was formerly followed by ''as'' and ''that'' used redundantly. See ''whenas.''}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Whan that'' Aprille with his shoures soote
The droghte of Marche hath perced to the roote.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Gen. Prol. to C. T., l. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Qwene that'' the kynge Arthur by conqueste hade wonnyne Castelles and kyngdoms, and contreez many.
{{right|''Morte Arthure'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 26.}}}}
{{smaller|''When'' is often used as a quasi-pronoun, meaning ‘which time,’ introducing a dependent clause after ''since, till'', or similar connective denoting time.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Shortly . . . I’ll resolve you, . . .
These happen’d accidents; ''till when'', be cheerful.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, v. 1. 250.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Since when'', his brain that had before been dry,
Became the well-spring of all poetry.</poem>
{{right|''Sir J. Davies'', Dancing.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Thy steeds will pause at even — ''till when'', farewell.</poem>
{{right|''Shelley'', Prometheus Unbound, iii. 2.}}}}
'''When all comes to all.''' See ''all.''

'''whenas''' (hwen-az′), ''conj.'' [< ''when'' + ''as''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' When. [Archaic.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Come, give me now a bag for my bread, . . .
And one for a peny, ''whenas'' I get any.</poem>
{{right|''Little John and the Four Beggars'' (Child’s Ballads, V. 326).}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Whenas'' in silks my Julia goes,
Till then, methinks, how sweetly flows
That liquefaction of her clothes!</poem>
{{right|''Herrick'', Upon Julia’s Clothes.}}}}
