readings.c2s1:
¶
'''2.''' Whereas; while. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|''Whenas,'' if they would enquire into themselves, they would find no such matter.
{{right|''Barrow.''}}}}
{{smaller block|Fit professors indeed are they like to be to teach others that godlinesse with content is great gaine, ''whenas'' their godlinesse of teaching had not been but for worldly gaine.
{{right|''Milton'', On Def. of Humb. Remonst.}}}}

'''whence''' (hwens), ''adv.'' and ''conj.'' [< ME. ''whens'', ''whennes'', ''whannes'', ''huannes'', with adv. gen. ''-es'', < ''whenne'', whence: see ''whenne''<sup>2</sup>.] '''I.''' ''interrog. adv.'' From what place? from what source, origin, or antecedents?
{{smaller block|''First Outlaw. Whence'' came you?<br>''Val.'' From Milan.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., iv. 1. 18.}}}}

'''II.''' ''rel. conj.'' From what place; from which place or source.
{{smaller block|Thes gost [spirit] him sseweth huet he is, . . . and ''huannes'' he comth, and huyder he geth.
{{right|''Ayenbite of Inwyt'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 115.}}}}
{{smaller block|I wot wel what ȝe ar & ''whennes'' ȝe come.
{{right|''William of Palerne'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 3122.}}}}
{{smaller block|Look unto the rock ''whence'' ye are hewn, and to the hole of the pit ''whence'' ye are digged.
{{right|Isa. li. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|Now wee may perceave the root of his hatred ''whence'' it springs.
{{right|''Milton'', Eikonoklastes, iv.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>We know not ''whence'' we live,
Or why, or how.</poem>
{{right|''Shelley'', Revolt of Islam, ix. 33.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Here was square keep, there turret high, . . .
''Whence'' oft the Warder could descry
The gathering ocean-storm.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', Marmion, v. 33.}}}}

'''From whence''', whence: a common pleonasm.
{{smaller block|''From whence'' come wars and fightings among ye?
{{right|Jas. iv. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>A place
''From whence'' himself does fly.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, iv. 2. 8.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|O, how unlike the place ''from whence'' they fell.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., i. 75.}}}}
'''Of whence''', whence: a pleonasm. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>He asked his airy guide,
What and ''of whence'' was he, who pressed the hero’s side.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Æneid, vi. 1193.}}}}

'''whence-ever''' (hwens-ev′ėr), ''conj.'' [< ''whence'' + ''ever.''] Whencesoever. ''Prior.'' (''Worcester.'') [Rare.]

'''whenceforth†''' (hwens-fōrth′), ''conj.'' [< ''whence'' + ''forth''<sup>1</sup>.] Forth from which place; whence. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Before them stands the God of Seas in place, . . .
And strikes the rockes with his three-forked mace;
''Whenceforth'' issues a warlike steed in sight.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', Muiopotmos, l. 316.}}}}

'''whencesoever''' (hwens-sō-ev′ėr), ''conj.'' [Early mod. E. ''whens-soever''; < ''whence'' + ''so''<sup>1</sup> + ''ever.''] From what place soever; from what cause or source soever.
{{smaller block|This Cytie of Jherusalem is in a fayre emynent place, for it stondeth vpon suche a grounde that from ''whens soever'' a man commyth thede he must nede ascende.
{{right|''Sir R. Guylforde'', Pylgrymage, p. 22.}}}}
{{smaller block|Any idea, ''whencesoever'' we have it. {{right|''Locke.''}}}}

'''whene’er''' (hwen-ãr′), ''conj.'' A contracted form of ''whenever.''

'''whenever''' (hwen-ev′ėr), ''conj.'' [< ME. ''when ever''; < ''when'' + ''ever.''] At whatever time; at what time soever.
{{smaller block|<poem>Ser, on to hir loggyng,
''When euer'' it please yow, I shall be your gyde;
ffor she is here by vppon the Ryuerez side.</poem>
{{right|''Generydes'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1245.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
''hwær'', < AS. ''hwær'', ''hwār'' = OS. ''hwār'', ''huār'' = OFries. ''hwēr'' = D. ''waar'' = MLG. ''wār'', ''wōr'', LG. ''waar'', ''woor'' = OHG. ''wār'', ''hwār'', MHG. ''wār-'', G. ''war-'' (in comp., as in ''war-um'', ''wor-in''), also reduced, OHG. MHG. ''wā'', G. ''wo'' = Icel. Sw. ''hvar'' = Dan. ''hvor'' = Goth. ''hwar'', where?; cf. Lith. ''kur'', where? L. ''cur'', OL. ''quor'', sometimes ''cor'' (usually explained as a contraction of ''quā re''), why? Skt. ''karhi'', at what time? when?; from the pronominal base represented by ''who'', ''what'': see ''who'', ''what''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''there'', as related to ''the'', ''that''.] '''I.''' ''interrog. adv.'' '''1.''' At or in what place? in what position, situation, or circumstances?
{{smaller block|''Hwer'' scule [shall] we win [wine] finden?
{{right|''Old Eng. Hom.'' (ed. Morris), I. 241.}}
If there were no opposition, ''where'' were the triall of an unfained goodnesse and magnanimity?
{{right|''Milton'', Church-Government, i. 7.}}
''Where'' sooner than here, ''where'' louder than here, may we expect a patriotic voice to be raised?
{{right|''D. Webster'', Speech, New York, March 10, 1831.}}}}
'''2.''' To which place? whither?
{{smaller block|<poem>''Where'' is bicome Cesar, that lorde was of al;
Or the riche man clothid in purpur & in pal?</poem>
{{right|''Hymns to Virgin'', etc. (E. E. T. S.), p. 86.}}
''Where'' runn’st thou so fast? {{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., iii. 2. 71.}}}}
'''3.''' From what source? whence?
{{smaller block|<poem>''Where'' have they this mettle?
Is not their climate foggy, raw and dull?</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., iii. 5. 15.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Where away?''' (''naut.''), a query from the officer of the deck as to the direction of any object reported by the lookout.}}

'''II.''' ''rel. conj.'' '''1.''' At or in which place, or the place in which; in which case, position, circumstances, etc.

readings.c3s2:
¶
{{smaller block|Asketh him Hwat beo ordre, and ''hwar'' he ifinde in holi write religiun openluker descriued. ''Ancren Riwle'', p. 8.}}

{{smaller block|He enforces hym to seke Ihesu in the joy of the worlde, ''whare'' neuer he sall be fundene.
{{right|''Hampole'', Prose Treatises (E. E. T. S.), p. 5.}}}}

{{smaller block|Bare ruin’d choirs, ''where'' late the sweet birds sang.
{{right|''Shak.'', Sonnets, lxxiii.}}}}

'''2.''' To which place; whither; to a place such that.
{{smaller block|<poem>Oh, cousin! thou hast led me ''where'' I never
Shall see day more. ''Shirley'', The Wedding, ii. 2.</poem>}}
'''3.''' Wherever.
{{smaller block|''Where'' the lordes and cheif men wax soe barbarous and bastardlike, what shall be hoped of the pesantes?
{{right|''Spenser'', State of Ireland.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Where'' your treasure is, there will your heart be also.
{{right|Mat. vi. 21.}}}}

{{smaller block|Now ''where'' nothing is, there nothing can come to be.
{{right|''J. Behme'', Aurora, xix. 438.}}}}
'''4.''' Whereas.
{{smaller block|<poem>His [Armagnac’s] wealth doth warrant a liberal dower,
''Where'' Reignier sooner will receive than give.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., v. 5. 47.}}}}

{{smaller block|It was observed that those who were born after the Beginning of this Mortality [the plague] had but twenty-eight Teeth, ''where'' before they had two and thirty.
{{right|''Baker'', Chronicles, p. 131.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Where'', frequently having the force or function of a relative or other pronoun (''which'', ''what'', etc.), is often used in composition with a following preposition: as, ''whereby'', ‘by what,’ ‘by which’; ''wherewith'', ‘with what,’ ‘with which.’ It was also formerly used after certain adverbs or adjectives in a general sense, as it still is in ''everywhere'', ''somewhere'' (which see), Middle English ''widen-wher'' (astray, at random), in forms corresponding to similar compounds of ''there'' (see ''there'').}}

{{smaller block|Thus I wente ''wyden-wher'', Dowel to seche.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (A), ix. 53.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
¶

'''where'''<sup>1</sup>'''†''' (hwãr), ''n.'' [Formerly also ''wheare;'' < ''where''<sup>1</sup>, ''adv.'', as used in ''everywhere, somewhere.''] Whereabout; situation; place.
{{smaller block|Finding the Nymph asleepe in secret ''wheare.''
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., III. iv. 19.}}
<poem>Bid them farewell, Cordelia, though unkind:
Thou losest here, a better ''where'' to find.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, i. 1. 264.}}}}

'''where'''<sup>2</sup>'''†''', ''conj.'' [< ME. ''wher, where'', contraction of ''wheder'', E. ''whether''<sup>1</sup>.] A contracted form of ''whether''<sup>1</sup>.
{{smaller block|''Wher'' he [the cat] ryt other rest other romyth to playe.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), i. 186.}}
<poem>Off hir linage enquered I no-thing;
''Where'' she be of duk or of markois hy,
Forsoth I wyll hyr haue, she is me pleasyng.</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 850.}}
<poem>I know not ''whēr'' I am or no; or speak,
Or whether thou dost hear me.</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', New Inn, v. 1.}}}}

'''whereabout''' (hwãr′a-bout′), ''adv.'' and ''conj.'' [< ''where''<sup>1</sup> + ''about.''] '''I.''' ''interrog. adv.'' About what? concerning what? near what or which place? as, ''whereabout'' did you drop the coin?

'''II.''' ''rel. conj.'' About which; concerning which; on what purpose.
{{smaller block|Let no man know anything of the business ''whereabout'' I send thee. {{right|1 Sam. xxi. 2.}}
<poem>I must not have you henceforth question me
Whither I go, nor reason ''whereabout.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., ii. 3. 107.}}}}
